<section class="section" id="s6">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-num">06</span>
    <h2>Strategic Recommendations</h2>
  </div>

  <p style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:28px;">Each recommendation is directly grounded in a specific finding from the dataset analysis above.</p>

  <div class="rec-grid">
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 01 — Talent Investment</div>
      <h4>Prioritize Deep Learning &amp; Python Talent Pipelines</h4>
      <p>Allocate a disproportionate share of talent acquisition budgets toward Deep Learning and Python specialists. These two skills produce Class-2 survival outcomes at 88.9% and 57.9% respectively — far above the 42% dataset average — while commanding salary premiums consistent with the Low Risk tier.</p>
      <div class="evidence"><strong>Data Basis:</strong> Skills Survival Analysis (Section 04) — Deep Learning Senior holders achieve 95.9% Class-2 survival; Python Senior Masters achieve 82.4%.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 02 — Reskilling Programs</div>
      <h4>Urgently Reskill Business &amp; Data Analysts</h4>
      <p>Business Analysts (risk score 0.85 by 2035) and Data Analysts (0.75) have zero Class-2 survival outcomes in the dataset. Reskilling programs should target transitions to Cloud Engineering, DevOps, or Cybersecurity — all of which have risk scores below 0.35 and survival rates above 50%.</p>
      <div class="evidence"><strong>Data Basis:</strong> Both BA and DA roles show 0% Class-2 survival and the highest risk trajectories in the dataset. Combined, they account for ~66.7M job openings — the largest at-risk labor pool.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 03 — Compensation Strategy</div>
      <h4>Anchor Compensation to Risk Exposure</h4>
      <p>Revise salary bands to explicitly reward lower-automation-risk skills. The data shows a persistent and widening 57–60% salary premium for Low Risk vs. High Risk jobs. High-demand, low-risk talent (AI Researchers, ML Engineers) requires premium compensation to attract and retain against rising market competition.</p>
      <div class="evidence"><strong>Data Basis:</strong> Low Risk median $34.1K vs. High Risk $21.7K; gap expands to ~$25K by 2035 (Section 03 — Salary by Risk Category × Year).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 04 — Geographic Strategy</div>
      <h4>Reassess India Workforce Allocation</h4>
      <p>India's workforce faces compounding risks: lowest median salary ($12.4K), highest automation risk score (0.401), and only 8.8% Class-2 survival. Any nearshoring or offshoring strategies heavily reliant on India-based analyst roles should include a phased reskilling transition plan targeting Cloud, Security, and DevOps pathways.</p>
      <div class="evidence"><strong>Data Basis:</strong> Country survival analysis (Section 05) — India's 8.8% Class-2 rate vs. 58.1% USA. Risk score nearly identical to other countries, confirming the issue is role mix, not geography.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 05 — Hiring Criteria</div>
      <h4>Deprioritize Academic Credentials; Prioritize Seniority &amp; Skill Depth</h4>
      <p>The near-zero salary differentiation between Bachelor ($31.6K), Master ($31.4K), and PhD ($31.7K) holders indicates that academic credentials have little labor market impact in this field. Instead, Senior experience level amplifies survival rates dramatically across all skills. Hiring and promotion criteria should weight demonstrated seniority and technical skill depth over degree type.</p>
      <div class="evidence"><strong>Data Basis:</strong> Education salary comparison (Section 03); Senior Deep Learning holders achieve 95.9% Class-2 survival regardless of degree (Section 04, Best Combinations table).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 06 — Workforce Planning</div>
      <h4>Plan for 16%+ At-Risk Worker Share by 2035</h4>
      <p>The at-risk (Class-0) workforce share is projected to reach 16.3% by 2035, up from 8.9% in 2015. Organizations should begin now to identify Class-0 workers in their workforce, map them to viable reskilling pathways (Deep Learning, Docker, AWS, Security), and build multi-year transition programs rather than reacting to displacement events.</p>
      <div class="evidence"><strong>Data Basis:</strong> Survival class year-over-year analysis (Section 05) — Class-0 share 8.9% in 2015–2016 vs. 16.3% in 2035.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

